    "id": "diabetes_primary_care_provider_additional_0",
    "title": "Additional Diabetes Management Strategy for Primary Care Providers",
    "domain": "Diabetes",
    "role": "Nurse Care Manager",
    "implementation_guidance": "1. Implement a targeted approach to diabetes management that includes: 1) Conduct comprehensive assessments; 2) Develop personalized care plans; 3) Provide culturally appropriate education; 4) Coordinate with specialists; 5) Monitor outcomes regularly; 6) Address social determinants of health; 7) Follow evidence-based protocols; 8) Engage patients and families; 9) Document interventions and outcomes; 10) Follow up to ensure adherence and address barriers.\n\u2022 Utilize point-of-care HbA1c testing with target values of <7.0% for most patients, <8.0% for elderly or those with comorbidities.\n\u2022 Implement SMBG (Self-Monitoring of Blood Glucose) protocol with testing frequency of 1-4 times daily based on medication regimen.\n\u2022 Apply validated risk stratification using UKPDS or ACCORD criteria to determine intervention intensity.\n\u2022 Document baseline and follow-up metrics including HbA1c, lipid panel, microalbumin/creatinine ratio, and estimated GFR.\n\u2022 Establish care plan with specific medication titration parameters based on fasting glucose values (target 80-130 mg/dL).\n\u2022 Implement standardized foot examination using 10g monofilament testing at 5 anatomical sites.\n\u2022 Schedule follow-up intervals based on risk stratification: high-risk q3mo, moderate-risk q6mo, low-risk annually.\n\u2022 Conduct standardized symptom assessment using validated tools with specific escalation thresholds and intervention pathways.\n\u2022 Apply evidence-based treatment algorithm with specific decision points, intervention selection criteria, and escalation pathways.\n\u2022 Define specific outcome targets with timeframes, measurement parameters, and adjustment thresholds.\n\u2022 Monitor quality metrics with defined process and outcome measures, data collection protocols, and performance thresholds\n\u2022 Implement protocol with specific timeframes: initial assessment within 7 days, follow-up at 30, 60, and 90 days, with comprehensive reassessment at 6 months.\n\u2022 Utilize standardized documentation template with required elements including assessment findings, intervention details, and outcome measures.",
    "expected_outcomes": "Improved diabetes outcomes, reduced complications, enhanced quality of life, decreased healthcare utilization, improved patient satisfaction, better treatment adherence, increased self-management skills, reduced healthcare disparities.",
    "target_population": "Medicaid-enrolled individuals from underserved communities, particularly those facing barriers to healthcare access.",
    "citation": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Evidence-Based Diabetes Management Guidelines for Medicaid Populations. https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/quality-of-care/quality-improvement-initiatives/. Published 2023. Accessed May 29, 2025.",
