    "id": "hypertension_primary_care_provider_additional_8",
    "title": "Additional Hypertension Management Strategy for Primary Care Providers",
    "domain": "Hypertension",
    "role": "Nurse Care Manager",
    "implementation_guidance": "1. Implement a targeted approach to hypertension management that includes: 1) Conduct comprehensive assessments; 2) Develop personalized care plans; 3) Provide culturally appropriate education; 4) Coordinate with specialists; 5) Monitor outcomes regularly; 6) Address social determinants of health; 7) Follow evidence-based protocols; 8) Engage patients and families; 9) Document interventions and outcomes; 10) Follow up to ensure adherence and address barriers.\n\u2022 Implement standardized BP measurement protocol using validated devices with appropriate cuff size (width 40% of arm circumference).\n\u2022 Target BP values: <130/80 mmHg for most patients, <140/90 mmHg for elderly (>65 years).\n\u2022 Utilize DASH dietary approach with sodium restriction to <2,300 mg/day.\n\u2022 Apply medication titration algorithm based on renin-angiotensin system blockade as first-line therapy.\n\u2022 Implement home BP monitoring protocol with minimum 14 readings/week (morning and evening).\n\u2022 Document medication adherence using validated PDC (Proportion of Days Covered) metric with target >80%.\n\u2022 Schedule follow-up intervals based on BP control: uncontrolled q2-4wk, controlled q3-6mo.\n\u2022 Apply risk stratification tool with defined intervention intensity based on risk level (high: weekly contact, moderate: biweekly, low: monthly).\n\u2022 Conduct standardized symptom assessment using validated tools with specific escalation thresholds and intervention pathways.\n\u2022 Apply evidence-based treatment algorithm with specific decision points, intervention selection criteria, and escalation pathways.\n\u2022 Define specific outcome targets with timeframes, measurement parameters, and adjustment thresholds.\n\u2022 Monitor quality metrics with defined process and outcome measures, data collection protocols, and performance thresholds\n\u2022 Implement protocol with specific timeframes: initial assessment within 7 days, follow-up at 30, 60, and 90 days, with comprehensive reassessment at 6 months.\n\u2022 Utilize standardized documentation template with required elements including assessment findings, intervention details, and outcome measures.",
    "expected_outcomes": "Improved hypertension outcomes, reduced complications, enhanced quality of life, decreased healthcare utilization, improved patient satisfaction, better treatment adherence, increased self-management skills, reduced healthcare disparities.",
    "target_population": "Medicaid-enrolled individuals from underserved communities, particularly those facing barriers to healthcare access.",
    "citation": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Evidence-Based Hypertension Management Guidelines for Medicaid Populations. https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/quality-of-care/quality-improvement-initiatives/. Published 2023. Accessed May 29, 2025.",
